        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{4}{4 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nChoose the antiderivative. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2 x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. The outside factor is $4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=2$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{4}{4 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=2\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\arctan{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=2\\left(\\arctan{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n2 \\arctan{\\left(2 x \\right)}+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
