      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186064219,
        "id": "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287",
        "title": "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "67db3791-c5e5-531b-a6ec-eecedc347ed2",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3}{16 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nChoose the antiderivative. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=4 x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(4)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. The outside factor is $3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{4}$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{3}{16 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{3}{4}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\arctan{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{3}{4}\\left(\\arctan{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 \\arctan{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{4}+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
